{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0079","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What model does this paper propose to close the alignment quality gap in attention-based NMT?","answer":"The paper proposes a Supervised Attention based NMT (SA-NMT) model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0079","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which off-the-shelf word aligners does SA-NMT use to obtain training alignments in advance?","answer":"SA-NMT uses off-the-shelf aligners such as GIZA++ or fast_align to obtain alignments of the bilingual training corpus in advance."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0079","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How many Chinese-to-English translation tasks were used in the paper's experiments?","answer":"The experiments were conducted on two Chinese-to-English translation tasks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0079","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What activation function does the NMT decoder's RNN hidden state use?","answer":"It uses a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) as the activation function."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0079","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How is attention supervision incorporated into SA-NMT's training?","answer":"It is implemented as a regularization term added to the joint training objective."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"The Twitter-Network (TN) topic model jointly models text and the social network in a full Bayesian nonparametric way.","source_span":"we propose the Twitter-Network (TN) topic model to jointly model the text and the social network in a full Bayesian nonparametric way"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"The TN topic model employs hierarchical Poisson-Dirichlet processes (PDP) for text modeling.","source_span":"The TN topic model employs the hierarchical Poisson-Dirichlet processes (PDP) for text modeling"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"The TN topic model uses a Gaussian process random function model for social network modeling.","source_span":"a Gaussian process random function model for social network modeling"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"Tweets are limited to 140 characters, which encourages informal words and abbreviations.","source_span":"due to the 140 characters limit"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"LDA yields poor results on tweets because it is heavily dependent on word co-occurrence, and tweets are short and noisy.","source_span":"LDA fails on short tweets since it is heavily dependent on word co-occurrence"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"Twitter-LDA and the behavior-topic model are not admixture models because they limit each document to one topic.","source_span":"Both models are not admixture models since they limit one topic per document"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"Hierarchical Dirichlet process (HDP) LDA was proposed to avoid the need to choose the number of topics in advance, using the Dirichlet process as a nonparametric prior.","source_span":"BIBREF9 proposed Hierarchical Dirichlet process (HDP) LDA, which utilizes the Dirichlet process (DP) as nonparametric prior"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","statement":"In the TN topic model, authorship information connects the text/hashtag component with the followers-network component.","source_span":"The authorship information serves to connect the two together."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What two Bayesian nonparametric components make up the Twitter-Network (TN) topic model?","answer":"It combines hierarchical Poisson-Dirichlet processes (PDP) for text modeling with a Gaussian process random function model for social network modeling."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What character limit on tweets does the paper mention as a source of informal language?","answer":"The paper mentions Twitter's 140-character limit as a reason tweets contain informal, abbreviated words."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Why does standard LDA perform poorly when applied directly to tweets?","answer":"Because LDA is heavily dependent on word co-occurrence, which is scarce in short, noisy tweets."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What limitation do Twitter-LDA and the behavior-topic model share that keeps them from being admixture models?","answer":"Both restrict each tweet/document to only one topic, so they are not admixture models."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What nonparametric prior does HDP LDA use to avoid having to fix the number of topics in advance?","answer":"HDP LDA uses the Dirichlet process (DP) as a nonparametric prior."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0080","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What links the text/hashtag modeling component to the follower-network modeling component in the TN topic model?","answer":"Authorship information connects the two components together."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"The paper introduces automatic drunk-texting prediction as the task of identifying whether a text was written under the influence of alcohol.","source_span":"we introduce automatic `drunk-texting prediction' as a computational task. Given a tweet, the goal is to automatically identify if it was written by a drunk user"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"Hashtag-based distant supervision is used to label tweets as drunk or sober for dataset creation.","source_span":"We use hashtag-based supervision so that the authors of the tweets mention if they were drunk at the time of posting a tweet"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"The classifiers used are SVM-based, relying on N-gram and stylistic features such as capitalisation and spelling errors.","source_span":"we present SVM-based classifiers that use N-gram and stylistic features such as capitalisation, spelling errors, etc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"Three datasets were created, each using a different strategy for selecting sober tweets.","source_span":"we create three datasets, each using a different strategy for sober tweets"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"Tweets shorter than 6 words in length, and tweets containing hyperlinks, were eliminated from the dataset.","source_span":"eliminate tweets that contain hyperlinks and also tweets that are shorter than 6 words in length"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"POS tags and named entity mentions used as features are obtained from NLTK.","source_span":"The POS tags and named entity mentions are obtained from NLTK BIBREF9"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"Spelling errors are identified as a feature using a spell checker by enchant.","source_span":"Spelling errors are identified using a spell checker by enchant"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"An LDA model with 20 topics was estimated, and the top 10 words per topic for both classes were used, resulting in 400 LDA-specific unigram features.","source_span":"we also estimated 20 topics for the dataset by estimating an LDA model BIBREF10 . We then consider top 10 words per topic, for both classes. This results in 400 LDA-specific unigrams that are then used as features."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"The repeated characters feature captures words containing a letter repeated three or more times, such as in 'happpy'.","source_span":"The repeated characters feature captures a situation in which a word contains a letter that is repeated three or more times, as in the case of happpy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","statement":"Five-fold cross-validation was used to evaluate classifier performance on Datasets 1 and 2.","source_span":"we train SVM classifiers BIBREF11 . We show the five-fold cross-validation performance of our features on Datasets 1 and 2"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What task does this paper introduce regarding tweets and alcohol use?","answer":"It introduces automatic drunk-texting prediction: the task of identifying whether a tweet was written by someone under the influence of alcohol."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How are tweets labeled as drunk or sober for building the dataset?","answer":"Tweets are labeled using hashtag-based distant supervision, where authors' own hashtags indicate whether they were drunk when posting."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What kind of classifier and features are used for drunk-texting prediction?","answer":"SVM-based classifiers are used, with N-gram features and stylistic features like capitalisation and spelling errors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What length and content criteria were used to filter out tweets when creating the dataset?","answer":"Tweets containing hyperlinks and tweets shorter than 6 words in length were eliminated from the dataset."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What tool is used to detect spelling errors as a stylistic feature in this study?","answer":"A spell checker provided by the 'enchant' library is used to identify spelling errors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How many LDA topics were estimated and how many resulting unigram features were used?","answer":"20 LDA topics were estimated, and taking the top 10 words per topic for both classes produced 400 LDA-specific unigram features."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0081","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What evaluation method was used to assess the SVM classifiers' performance?","answer":"Five-fold cross-validation was used to evaluate the classifiers' performance."}
